Fixing Antigravity IDE on Legacy CPUs (Arch Linux)
The Problem
Antigravity IDE (and its language server) requires AES-NI CPU instructions. If your CPU doesn't support them (common on older Intel/AMD chips), the IDE will crash with SIGILL (Illegal Instruction) or Signal 4. On Arch Linux, this also manifests as a "Code 127" error if the necessary emulator isn't found.
The Fix: QEMU User-Mode Emulation
1. Install Requirements
You need the QEMU user-mode emulator and some compatibility libraries for Arch:
bash

sudo pacman -S qemu-user qemu-user-binfmt libxcrypt-compat xdg-utils

Use code with caution.
2. Locate the Binary
Find where the language server is installed. Usually:
/opt/Antigravity/resources/app/extensions/antigravity/bin/language_server_linux_x64
3. Apply the Shim
Move the original binary and replace it with a script that emulates the missing instructions:
bash

# Navigate to the bin directory
cd /opt/Antigravity/resources/app/extensions/antigravity/bin/

# Rename the original binary
sudo mv language_server_linux_x64 language_server_linux_x64.real

# Create the shim script
sudo nano language_server_linux_x64

Use code with caution.
Paste the following into the script:
bash

#!/bin/bash
DIR="$(cd "$(dirname "${BASH_SOURCE}")" && pwd)"
# Emulates a Westmere CPU (which supports AES-NI) using the absolute path to QEMU
exec /usr/bin/qemu-x86_64 -cpu Westmere "$DIR/language_server_linux_x64.real" "$@"

Use code with caution.
4. Set Permissions
bash

sudo chmod +x language_server_linux_x64
sudo chmod +x language_server_linux_x64.real

Use code with caution.
5. Launch the IDE
If you encounter UI issues or unclickable buttons, launch with these flags to bypass sandbox/GPU conflicts:
bash

antigravity --no-sandbox --disable-gpu --ozone-platform=x11

Use code with caution.
Why this works
The shim intercepts the IDE's call to the language server and runs it through qemu-x86_64. By specifying -cpu Westmere, we tell QEMU to provide a virtual CPU that supports the AES instructions the binary expects, even though the physical hardware lacks them.
